n/id #uuid "f5065c71-e9f3-5dc3-a977-b3142c1564e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-108911" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}+y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}$ $$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={y}^{6}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=-1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,q(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,n=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-108911/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-108911"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-108918/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b457a82e-67b3-5fc3-a12b-ae3bdf0fd844" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108918/selection/3c280d10825df4c383894d747cc3c5d9dbe51ab7964aebe66e34a65044753c9e" :answer/id #uuid "c8cb3f35-11d1-59fd-bab3-f624dad92f7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108918/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "5d9e8f08-78ad-5798-9478-d0ac1981cb43" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108918/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "025cf3b0-67a6-537c-9ed6-899fb7d59c92" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108918/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e34c8c0b-b3e9-5916-b6ab-09ad81baaa3a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108918/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "84220b9f-402b-5cda-9db8-aac0dc695f09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-108918/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-108918/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-108918/selection/3c280d10825df4c383894d747cc3c5d9dbe51ab7964aebe66e34a65044753c9e" "q-108918/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-108918/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-108918/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-108918" :question/id #uuid "8de450ab-0663-5275-b98e-533b8807111a" :question/math-academy-id "q-108918" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$\n- $y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={cos}^{2}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={y}^{-5/2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation.  If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{y})=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡y}{y}.\n$$\n\nIt is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-108918/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-108918"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-108913/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "f113985d-f65e-582a-9884-207ac27f7767" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108913/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "6fb5252b-dfeb-5b63-910b-338a3e51c6a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108913/selection/3c280d10825df4c383894d747cc3c5d9dbe51ab7964aebe66e34a65044753c9e" :answer/id #uuid "60d941f7-1f89-5873-8892-fe050a1f0488" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108913/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "da5e6a4d-2f1b-5a20-add2-fc4099ec61dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108913/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "7f1bbe9e-b079-52a2-8902-daf72c9392b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108913/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9584848-1801-5354-bc4b-49fba689114b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-108913/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-108913/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-108913/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-108913/selection/3c280d10825df4c383894d747cc3c5d9dbe51ab7964aebe66e34a65044753c9e" "q-108913/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-108913/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-108913" :question/id #uuid "68ed3341-b50d-54ad-bfb9-a488c4f940ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-108913" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n- ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}y={x}^{2}{y}^{5}$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-x{y}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-{e}^{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=5.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-108913/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-108913"] :db/ensure [: