9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-108916" :question/id #uuid "b849caa5-cdf1-5e8c-9248-6bcaf7e0f549" :question/math-academy-id "q-108916" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}-x{y}^{5}={y}^{4}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{3}={y}^{2}\n$$\n\nand this is not a Bernoulli differential equation because it is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\n$$\n\nand this is not a Bernoulli differential equation because it is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x){y}^{n}$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-108916/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-108916"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-108914/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "6905ad05-d767-5812-b2d5-cf584ad2c9ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108914/selection/3c280d10825df4c383894d747cc3c5d9dbe51ab7964aebe66e34a65044753c9e" :answer/id #uuid "89e17a45-db1c-5651-adaf-48b556c97013" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108914/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "c8420ab7-fa75-54fa-b7a7-8035696e697c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108914/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "38541cf7-518f-594f-abd0-73d948b4584c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108914/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "05b91f6e-5ea2-5fcc-b287-062019742750" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108914/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "03c93ccf-a7d0-52e1-a88d-adc3b883208c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-108914/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-108914/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-108914/selection/3c280d10825df4c383894d747cc3c5d9dbe51ab7964aebe66e34a65044753c9e" "q-108914/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-108914/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-108914/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-108914" :question/id #uuid "d5827e29-bd33-5f0e-a7fa-1b43ad635551" :question/math-academy-id "q-108914" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=x{y}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-108914/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-108914"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109014/selection/1a068f071c3079753a9622ee0b383e1e35b7c35ec168971f53d6b73d15208b4f" :answer/id #uuid "60f672be-59eb-5b38-b7e5-85ac921a523c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109014/selection/2d6cfdfd554881720879744154242fb5aa6f8fe5d2ee6c2ca18d39657060f0e9" :answer/id #uuid "38405260-ab6f-51a0-b17d-f56c264e1119" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109014/selection/ef39ac684c1e3e373db0790bbe4fb3c21610f65a536c8a81728af1a98d8cc216" :answer/id #uuid "03bc8c21-bc42-5449-8f50-19177bb9c2d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109014/selection/07c71ea73bb8110bc7a6da95b843ea949731374fc7b33be3dc0291a2b916365d" :answer/id #uuid "ec9ebd43-6d7d-5d17-885f-3f08b160bed6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109014/selection/1ff101761a1e5581bf65e642cb9e28a5c6545803559d69239323704698f00336" :answer/id #uuid "d928e2ae-ed12-5d9e-af9a-d31b01f55957" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109014/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "31d4b6eb-fe21-5eb6-8c7c-d4e95278152b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109014/selection/1a068f071c3079753a9622ee0b383e1e35b7c35ec168971f53d6b73d15208b4f" :answer-field/choices ["q-109014/selection/1a068f071c3079753a9622ee0b383e1e35b7c35ec168971f53d6b73d15208b4f" "q-109014/selection/2d6cfdfd554881720879744154242fb5aa6f8fe5d2ee6c2ca18d39657060f0e9" "q-109014/selection/ef39ac684c1e3e373db0790bbe4fb3c21610f65a536c8a81728af1a98d8cc216" "q-109014/selection/07c71ea73bb8110bc7a6da95b843ea949731374fc7b33be3dc0291a2b916365d" "q-109014/selection/1ff101761a1e5581bf65e642cb9e28a5c6545803559d69239323704698f00336"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109014" :question/id #uuid "dd8094aa-e7b0-59c9-acb1-1eafca183add" :question/math-academy-id "q-109014" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+3xy=-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3x,\\,q(x)=-{x}^{4},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+3x{y}^{-2}=-{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3xz=-{x}^{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-6xz=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-6x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109014/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109014"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109009/selection/17e7ff2de2ba7a85dabc74eddbb17d064b3affa008f12d2cf1c1f4623aa43b97" :answer/id #uuid "f884247f-c187-5c62-abff-c8b2a433e102" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109009/selection/9e4e898dbc31977e505143e198e1cfc7a4c17381889b07a708f9408632242dc2" :answer/id #uuid "1f7d0b71-b4fb-5475-ae0d-2063b12cd78a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109009/selection/e712e082d2d574106fe6217a023c50c8f14d84773500b4653009bfd49da323d9" :answer/id #uuid "9481bf6d-a8c2-58a6-8013-bbbc717e164e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109009/selection/243edc54dad7755d0753d38a589add34d7c9d01e89115dbe19945f670c34fd57" :answer/id #uuid "0a938b54-7bb4-5122-b4f3-af821a595a6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109009/selection/65724bc9bae40df5e196b4691f89a056045d33c2cbba15ecb5bb70f19c751645" :answer/id #uuid "9fbeaa9c-5c50-5fc0-804a-5e5aa4838165" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109009/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9406f410-f316-5318-ba6d-d3b4b7823619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109009/selection/9e4e898dbc31977e505143e198e1cfc7a4c17381889b07a708f9408632242dc2" :answer-field/choices ["q-109009/selection/17e7ff2de2ba7a85dabc74eddbb17d064b3affa008f12d2cf1c1f4623aa43b97" "q-109009/selection/9e4e898dbc31977e505143e198e1cfc7a4c17381889b07a708f9408632242dc2" "q-109009/selection/e712e082d2d574106fe6217a023c50c8f14d84773500b4653009bfd49da323d9" "q-109009/selection/243edc54dad7755d0753d38a589add34d7c9d01e89115dbe19945f670c34fd57" "q-109009/selection/65724bc9bae40df5e196b4691f89a056045d33c2cbba15ecb5bb70f19c751645"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109009" :question/id #uuid "116a868f-3341-5158-80f2-0367b6d92068" :question/math-academy-id "q-109009" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x={y}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,n=5.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{5},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-5}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-4{y}^{-5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-5}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+4z\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109009/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109009"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-106070"] :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y}{x}=x\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{{y}^{1/2}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{2x}\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-106071/selection/1e35142e190b3d963542c5d1493d8318c8d0d58172bbbacea4a93ab8f160ec44" :answer/id #uuid "3e7eb463-209e-5803-8685-e5a512aeba24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6{x}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106071/selection/f0d2263266c9cb2b07157876e84a0fa82f853503c791b7b72148a46a4c4082b3" :answer/id #uuid "c6dfe1ff-a514-5308-95c8-2bf5dcd15898" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6{x}^{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106071/selection/be2e6cc8987ef46de7d2ec11a672870b424b13727905e9e68042170360a51e63" :answer/id #uuid "9222a794-6bbc-523f-bd20-00c12a22dc00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106071/selection/5e28167fbf6c8139a11bc4632f7cd0765ad86318ca628e8daedf2c430b44795d" :answer/id #uuid "9f3820dc-6520-57a7-9280-7d5fefc81d71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106071/selection/8b023a7622929a70e2fe557191d76a824ace71b99cf75dbe7815c2573000490c" :answer/id #uuid "7230d4ca-3e31-58e4-a7bf-3b41dbfc0d98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{x}^{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106071/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c54109d3-e3be-5884-906e-34b15ffdbf65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106071/selection/1e35142e190b3d963542c5d1493d8318c8d0d58172bbbacea4a93ab8f160ec44" :answer-field/choices ["q-106071/selection/1e35142e190b3d963542c5d1493d8318c8d0d58172bbbacea4a93ab8f160ec44" "q-106071/selection/f0d2263266c9cb2b07157876e84a0fa82f853503c791b7b72148a46a4c4082b3" "q-106071/selection/be2e6cc8987ef46de7d2ec11a672870b424b13727905e9e68042170360a51e63" "q-106071/selection/5e28167fbf6c8139a11bc4632f7cd0765ad86318ca628e8daedf2c430b44795d" "q-106071/selection/8b023a7622929a70e2fe557191d76a824ace71b99cf75dbe7815c2573000490c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106071" :question/id #uuid "81e61808-2e93-5c92-87e6-c51f5a422b87" :question/math-academy-id "q-106071" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-y(2x+3{y}^{2})=0\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-y(2x+3{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-2xy-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-2xy & =3{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}y & =3{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)=3{x}^{-2},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-2}=3{x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z=3{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+4{x}^{-1}z=-6{x}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-6{x}^{-2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-106071/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106071"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-106069"] :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=x{y}^{4}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{-3}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}z=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-109177/selection/c675a9221f187857b214f951c14a2ebcf2137c7115a5397e9ef19c02a9afb148" :answer/id #uuid "1a0fe20f-d490-5e96-8fe8-13198ca13a46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109177/selection/6f6698258f7667541f37ffac98337cf6ae96729177a887793e899e2500b8e5bc" :answer/id #uuid "ab973e4e-44e4-5930-8f08-e443873c7255" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3+Cx)}^{2/3}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109177/selection/83577d4d1c200f89f272b95ec8bb883d0f410578a57d4507029613affa665835" :answer/id #uuid "9cb05e70-09c7-511c-a3da-b228de724fd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+C}{x})}^{2/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109177/selection/9923c74af829d8fda0b978c46ff2ac998528d8a60302a2b257dcaaa112d69862" :answer/id #uuid "6802132b-9c64-5e97-b9c3-807808dee09e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2/3}-Cx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109177/selection/48bbab67668ee16f428518b53811772b01254182f33338a295961a6ed669059f" :answer/id #uuid "ca6a71f1-f338-56a2-ba9d-2839cbc1a566" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{x}+C{x}^{2/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109177/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "09eeedea-68f8-5e46-9bc9-875227450c10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109177/selection/c675a9221f187857b214f951c14a2ebcf2137c7115a5397e9ef19c02a9afb148" :answer-field/choices ["q-109177/selection/c675a9221f187857b214f951c14a2ebcf2137c7115a5397e9ef19c02a9afb148" "q-109177/selection/6f6698258f7667541f37ffac98337cf6ae96729177a887793e899e2500b8e5bc" "q-109177/selection/83577d4d1c200f89f272b95ec8bb883d0f410578a57d4507029613affa665835" "q-109177/selection/9923c74af829d8fda0b978c46ff2ac998528d8a60302a2b257dcaaa112d69862" "q-109177/selection/48bbab67668ee16f428518b53811772b01254182f33338a295961a6ed669059f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109177" :question/id #uuid "a34e4e3f-41a3-551c-82a5-ec9f10547dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-109177" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-xy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{-1/2}-xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy & ={y}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{3}{2x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/2x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(3/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{3/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}z & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z) & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{3/2}z & =3{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{3/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{3/2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-109177/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109177"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109158/selection/ec7238a7e761ee2118261fb40ff468b9ada1353934821377aa96790c9c642640" :answer/id #uuid "0ac158c7-8037-5a04-ba89-9fa9f2e96f69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{C-{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109158/selection/b798b0abad631fd9d56f28e3e1b914f102e9d44eddfc03dfb7fd55cfecf32606" :answer/id #uuid "f8f266e6-06f4-5beb-9273-1dd7c3416602" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109158/selection/389d05fe7b0569dd2a6a24aa80ca445c82b0130491fae17812d8b24c7617bfde" :answer/id #uuid "40e8b2dd-3236-5ef1-abae-906e9c812973" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{C-x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109158/selection/5c2a3e11289c958e079ef5c9a27ecaad0fa3fa8d7af340a7d651f1223bfe93b6" :answer/id #uuid "58d1df92-1703-579d-8d17-7ee95aee5015" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{C+2{x}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109158/selection/55281f2656dbad5265bb2030de4bd735a1a3c0e0207aafe0a3a819700b4194f0" :answer/id #uuid "33dee3e6-1fd5-5d3d-8770-11c9d79eda96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109158/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "020c3836-2be9-5894-a776-42d19c589e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109158/selection/55281f2656dbad5265bb2030de4bd735a1a3c0e0207aafe0a3a819700b4194f0" :answer-field/choices ["q-109158/selection/ec7238a7e761ee2118261fb40ff468b9ada1353934821377aa96790c9c642640" "q-109158/selection/b798b0abad631fd9d56f28e3e1b914f102e9d44eddfc03dfb7fd55cfecf32606" "q-109158/selection/389d05fe7b0569dd2a6a24aa80ca445c82b0130491fae17812d8b24c7617bfde" "q-109158/selection/5c2a3e11289c958e079ef5c9a27ecaad0fa3fa8d7af340a7d651f1223bfe93b6" "q-109158/selection/55281f2656dbad5265bb2030de4bd735a1a3c0e0207aafe0a3a819700b4194f0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109158" :question/id #uuid "8cbfb9b8-f301-5858-97fc-1b90fa72d9ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-109158" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-109158/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109158"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109172/selection/203200328a2f43756dc192ebcfe3c899885d457674b7ab7490e481802efca0e5" :answer/id #uuid "c223b186-25ed-5556-ae81-832fa38010f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{Cx+2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109172/selection/79f69ad131795c6f3e599321b26420cecc41fdac454144b61ae8bf34c9b765ff" :answer/id #uuid "053ed39b-2844-5c61-bdf2-11e333f54a7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{C-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109172/selection/b95d875f8240b8c76707aed0867eb20879a4777037f7fc91195d5a8b9ab277eb" :answer/id #uuid "90c0c870-61ea-52b1-9b3f-cb0f8666d6e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{x+C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109172/selection/90a310d0a286f292e8f15e4ff716431e3d02e6fffea81ec0c7de0b3c705e7cad" :answer/id #uuid "ee335274-7218-5d42-9249-2944d9b71a86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109172/selection/b84055ed50f92caa8223860286f16c0043f601dd0cdf6bdb24f5070e5893de21" :answer/id #uuid "e1dc7e47-2cd2-5189-a166-6ef6c72fdeaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{C-x}{2{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109172/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "04defa10-b4f3-5f01-bed1-9311fd9923c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109172/selection/90a310d0a286f292e8f15e4ff716431e3d02e6fffea81ec0c7de0b3c705e7cad" :answer-field/choices ["q-109172/selection/203200328a2f43756dc192ebcfe3c899885d457674b7ab7490e481802efca0e5" "q-109172/selection/79f69ad131795c6f3e599321b26420cecc41fdac454144b61ae8bf34c9b765ff" "q-109172/selection/b95d875f8240b8c76707aed0867eb20879a4777037f7fc91195d5a8b9ab277eb" "q-109172/selection/90a310d0a286f292e8f15e4ff716431e3d02e6fffea81ec0c7de0b3c705e7cad" "q-109172/selection/b84055ed50f92caa8223860286f16c0043f601dd0cdf6bdb24f5070e5893de21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109172" :question/id #uuid "feae7a15-8f8f-5200-a9c1-501431f820f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109172" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-1}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z=-{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & =-x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}\\,.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-109172/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109172"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109026/selection/22764482833bffb3f739c41e7ba3d2dcc7e76d3e80caf96c1a7ca210cd92c3c6" :answer/id #uuid "27173a27-dbca-5684-9713-abfd2571ef62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109026/selection/cfa58cc4ba246aaf97b02aa9383a7187ac3a48c39a0b63435d26e71458e65f1a" :answer/id #uuid "20e427ab-888f-5588-8561-75a9cd8ddf51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109026/selection/fd3541208b516fc05fd2c9e279c77805cffe00d75b3f681055395735cc83aa61" :answer/id #uuid "f4ecef40-f699-5a5d-b6ec-c3854183b138" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}{C+x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109026/selection/0b95d1b8e49a7ceb0b64d847858cbfbf2815f2ec988d765d62fec098a81f7b3d" :answer/id #uuid "26249874-c894-5357-979d-b24370fb7c08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109026/selection/7dbf318bf3f0d7fa76f0cfc90ae892649ea23f922ed6d9452d08a4f4e4446eaf" :answer/id #uuid "e88af2c4-817c-578f-8a48-7c81e49fd450" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1+C{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109026/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c6a6a11-b932-534c-b176-0d494348c1fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109026/selection/cfa58cc4ba246aaf97b02aa9383a7187ac3a48c39a0b63435d26e71458e65f1a" :answer-field/choices ["q-109026/selection/22764482833bffb3f739c41e7ba3d2dcc7e76d3e80caf96c1a7ca210cd92c3c6" "q-109026/selection/cfa58cc4ba246aaf97b02aa9383a7187ac3a48c39a0b63435d26e71458e65f1a" "q-109026/selection/fd3541208b516fc05fd2c9e279c77805cffe00d75b3f681055395735cc83aa61" "q-109026/selection/0b95d1b8e49a7ceb0b64d847858cbfbf2815f2ec988d765d62fec098a81f7b3d" "q-109026/selection/7dbf318bf3f0d7fa76f0cfc90ae892649ea23f922ed6d9452d08a4f4e4446eaf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109026" :question/id #uuid "6dc285ec-042e-5dd7-8bf3-c6ac544a9a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-109026" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=x{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=x,\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+x{y}^{-1}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+xz=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-x,\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (-x) \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}) & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109026/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109026"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-106073"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)={e}^{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-1}={e}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=-{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-{e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9069"] :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n\n- $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n\n- $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = e^x y^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = -xy^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8963"] :question/problem "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8965"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x}  = \\dfrac{x^2}{y} .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
